    .contact { margin-bottom: 20px; }
    .date { margin-bottom: 20px; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <div class="contact">
    <p>Christos Hadjinikolis<br>
    christos.hadjinikolis@gmail.com<br>
    https://christos-hadjinikolis.github.io/<br>
    https://uk.linkedin.com/in/christoshadjinikolis</p>
  </div>

  <p class="date">31 July 2026</p>

  <p>Dear JetBrains hiring team,</p>

  <p>I am applying for the Head of ML role because the platform problem you describe is the problem my career has increasingly been about. Many ML leaders have deep experience in model development. My work has focused on the layer that decides whether model work becomes useful repeatedly: the organisations, operating models, developer workflows, and production platforms that turn research into dependable systems.</p>

  <p>At Vortexa, I lead the engineering strategy and delivery for a client-facing live streaming intelligence estate that processes roughly 6 million vessel-position records per hour into production intelligence. I lead a 10-person cross-functional team across ML engineering, data engineering, data science, Product, and domain expertise. The work sits in the same tension JetBrains calls out: balancing long-term R&amp;D with short-term product needs, while maintaining reliability, observability, governance, and measurable value for users.</p>

  <p>That has required more than model delivery. I established Vortexa's ML development lifecycle from research to production, including MLflow, model/data versioning, service-based deployment, replay/evaluation interfaces, and live accuracy monitoring. I led the multi-year move from Kafka Streams toward Apache Flink for critical live pipelines, using Java and Kotlin exposure from JVM streaming work while keeping the focus on maintainability, operational clarity, and independent scaling. I also introduced or shaped engineering practices around monitoring, runbooks, fallback paths, architecture forums, DORA/Jira delivery signals, Backstage adoption, and local integration testing. These mechanisms helped keep a business-critical system operable, with MTTR managed under 30 minutes and delivery signals such as 14.65 deploys/week with 0.52% change failure in the latest team view.</p>

  <p>The JetBrains vision also resonates because it treats AI in software development as a systems and governance problem, not simply a model problem. As AI becomes part of planning, building, reviewing, and operating software, teams need cost control, auditability, observability, workflow design, approval boundaries, and evidence that human and autonomous work can be coordinated safely. That is where I think my production ML, standards, and developer-tooling background is most relevant.</p>

  <p>I have built public tooling around those concerns. <strong>dynamicio</strong> is a PyPI library for making I/O seams and data contracts explicit so ML/data pipelines can be tested locally with faster feedback. <strong>skeleton-replay</strong> is a PyPI package that turns Python script and pytest runs into runtime traces, architecture snapshots, workflow evidence, and replayable reports for review, debugging, onboarding, and LLM-assisted understanding. I also published the <strong>Skeleton Replay</strong> plugin on the JetBrains Marketplace, bringing those reports and source-navigation workflows into PyCharm/IntelliJ. It is small compared with JetBrains' platform ambition, but it is a concrete signal: I care about developer experience, evidence, IDE workflow, and making complex systems understandable where developers already work.</p>

  <p>My standards work reinforces the same direction. As a UCL Department of Information Studies Research Associate and ISO/CEN-CENELEC JTC 21 committee expert, I work on practical AI standardisation: auditability, model/data versioning, explainability, safe adoption, and how standards can make responsible AI easier for companies rather than turning into bureaucracy. That perspective feels directly relevant to an AI-native developer platform that must work across teams, products, and environments without forcing companies into closed ecosystems.</p>

  <p>I recognise that the advertised scope is larger than my current direct-management scale. What I would bring is a specific combination of production ML depth, developer-platform instincts, cross-functional operating-model experience, and a record of shaping organisations beyond formal authority. I drove Vortexa's Data Production organisation from 4 to 30+ people by developing hiring loops, system-design culture, platform choices, and delivery discipline, while remaining close enough to the technology to set useful standards rather than abstract process.</p>

  <p>I would be excited to discuss how this experience could help JetBrains build the execution and governance layer for AI-driven software development.</p>

  <p>Sincerely,</p>

  <p>Christos Hadjinikolis</p>
</body>
</html>
